[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Attachments

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: HARNESS-ATTACHMENTS-1
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Attachment ingress through Jobs
STATEMENT: >>>
Attachment ingress and delivery share Jobs with the existing clients. Both
conversation_send and enqueue_job accept inline attachments plus attachment_ids;
inline values precede resolved IDs, with a combined maximum of eight and 4 MiB.
Empty text is accepted only with attachments. Text/image validation and native
provider mapping are reused. Upload alone never executes a provider turn.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/jobs.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/infrastructure/tests/attachments.rs

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: HARNESS-ATTACHMENTS-2
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Scoped upload identity and workspace snapshots
STATEMENT: >>>
Upload IDs belong to the canonical provider/account/workspace JobScope. A
normalized-content fingerprint reuses identical uploads, even at the 16-per-scope
or 128-per-host limit. Metadata listing omits bytes and source paths. Workspace
snapshots use FileContextReader: text supports one-based ranges (16 MiB source,
256 KiB selected text), and image reads stop at 2 MiB + 1 with no line ranges.
The existing HTTP text-context endpoint keeps its behavior. Path validation and
canonical containment reject traversal, Git metadata and links outside the root.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/jobs.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/infrastructure/tests/attachments.rs

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: HARNESS-ATTACHMENTS-3
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Atomic upload admission and deletion
STATEMENT: >>>
Uploads persist in jobs.sqlite3 until explicit deletion. Admission resolves IDs,
validates combined size, copies bytes into the job and commits its command
receipt in one SQLite transaction. A receipt is checked before resolving IDs,
so an identical retry can return a retained job after staging deletion. Fresh
commands cannot reference a deleted/foreign ID; failed validation creates neither
a job nor receipt. Upload deletion does not alter existing jobs or history.
Worktree removal also checks staged uploads under its workspace write lease;
delete them first so removal cannot orphan an inaccessible upload scope.
Existing empty-ID command fingerprints remain compatible with HTTP retries.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/jobs.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/infrastructure/tests/attachments.rs

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
``cargo test --locked -p gpui-agent-api --test jobs -- --attachments-only`` covers
authenticated inline/uploaded text and image delivery through all three native
fixtures, attachment-only messages, immutable snapshots/history, retries,
read-only and scope denials, invalid payloads and secret-free host diagnostics.
``--uploads-only`` runs just the upload cases. Repository restart, quota/dedup and
atomic aggregate-limit failures are covered by ``cargo harness test --package
gpui-agent-infrastructure --test attachments``. No live account inference is used.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: MCP transport and session credentials

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: HARNESS-MCP-SESSION-1
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Stateless Streamable HTTP endpoint
STATEMENT: >>>
The isolated server exposes ``/mcp`` using stateless Streamable HTTP with JSON
responses. Supported protocol revisions are 2025-03-26, 2025-06-18, and 2025-11-25.
GET returns 405 because this endpoint does not offer a server SSE stream.
Each POST accepts one JSON-RPC message; send ``Accept: application/json,
text/event-stream``, ``Content-Type: application/json``, and the negotiated
``MCP-Protocol-Version``. See the official
`transport <https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/basic/transports>`__
and `tool <https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools>`__
contracts used for this implementation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/jobs.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/mcp_sessions.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/infrastructure/tests/mcp_sessions.rs

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: HARNESS-MCP-SESSION-2
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Operator token handling
STATEMENT: >>>
The server prints token **paths**, not values. ``mcp-read.token`` grants only MCP
reads; the existing API token grants environment-wide write authority. These
operator credentials are never injected into providers.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/jobs.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/mcp_sessions.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/infrastructure/tests/mcp_sessions.rs

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: HARNESS-MCP-SESSION-3
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Scoped session bridge
STATEMENT: >>>
The server and embedded desktop automatically inject ``gpui-agent-session``, a
stdio bridge to the host's loopback MCP endpoint, into each provider process
running a prompt. Credentials are unique to that execution and bound to its
provider, account, and canonical workspace path. Sessions can read only matching
jobs, events and conversations owned by that scope. By default they cannot submit, cancel or update history. Start the owning
host with ``--mcp-write`` (or ``cargo harness dev --mcp-write``) to allow
these write operations. Ordinary jobs remain in the same scope. A remote desktop client cannot grant
permissions on its remote host. Omitted account/workspace arguments resolve to
the calling session, not another environment's default workspace.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/jobs.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/api/tests/mcp_sessions.rs
- TYPE: File
  VALUE: crates/infrastructure/tests/mcp_sessions.rs

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: HARNESS-MCP-SESSION-4
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Session token lifetime and revocation
STATEMENT: >>>
Tokens expire after 900 seconds using a monotonic clock, and are revoked when the
provider invocation completes, fails, times out, or is cancelled. Resume starts a
new process and mints a fresh token. Host shutdown revokes all tokens and closes
admission; restart creates a new in-memory registry. No session credentials are
stored in SQLite or profile settings, and debug output redacts the token.
Codex/Claude receive secrets through child-process environment variables; ACP
receives the bridge environment through its private session request. Arguments
contain only the host executable and ``--mcp-stdio``. The bridge never forwards
operator tokens, uses no proxy, and does not reauthenticate an expired credential.
Antigravity must advertise MCP stdio support; missing support fails the prompt
with an actionable error instead of silently removing the security boundary.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: File
